The grubs bore into the wood of trees or sometimes into the roots or pith of herbaceous plants.
These species are polyphagous, attacking many herbaceous crops, especially at the seedling stage.
While in Homoptera the wings are uniform, in Heteroptera the apical portion of the wings is more membraneous than the remainder.
Metamorphosis is usually gradual, and rarely complete.
This suborder comprises a very diverse group in which aphids, coccids (scale insects), whiteflies, leafhoppers, psyllids, plant-hoppers, spittle bugs, and lantern bugs are represented.
They have piercing-sucking mouthparts and usually undergo incomplete metamorphosis.
Nevertheless, their metamorphosis is sometimes complete, especially in males.
Pests belong to a broad spectrum of organisms including insects, mites, ticks , mice, rats, and other rodents, slugs, snails, nematodes, cestodes, weeds, fungi, bacteria, viruses.
The members of this suborder, in particular aphids and leafhoppers, have gained special importance because of their contribution as vectors in the transmission of plant viruses.
A distinctive feature of aphids, some scales, and other sucking insects is the production of honeydew.
This is the clear, sticky dropping that lands on the leaves or anything else below the plant or tree upon which aphids are feeding.
Honeydew-coated objects soon become covered by one or more black or brown fungi known as sooty molds.
In the following sections we present some summarized information on aphids, scale insects, and whiteflies.
Aphids are small (about 2 mm long), soft-bodied insects which attack nearly all indoor and outdoor plants including vegetables, field crops, and fruit trees.
There are hundreds of different species of aphids, some attacking only one plant and many attacking numerous ones.
A characteristic common to all species is the presence of two tubes, called cornicles, on the back ends of their bodies.
The cornicles secrete defensive substances.
The present article is confined to summary information on the most important insect pests that damage fruit trees and field crops in Persia.
In some species they are quite long, while in others they are very short and difficult to see.
Aphids feed by sucking up plant juices through a food channel in their beaks, while at the same time injecting saliva into the host.
While light infestations are usually not harmful to plants, higher aphid populations cause leaf curl, wilting, stunting of shoot growth, and delay in the production of flowers and fruit.
Stem mothers reproduce parthenogenetically, and their eggs are held within their bodies until they hatch so that young are born alive.
All offspring are females that soon mature and begin to reproduce in the same manner.
With the return of autumn’s shorter days and cooler temperatures, a generation appears that includes both males and females.
After mating, these females lay the fertilized eggs that overwinter and eventually hatch into stem mothers the following spring.
Forest insects, stored-product insects, and insects that damage livestock are not treated here.
These insects are a peculiar group which look quite different from typical insects.
They are small, immobile, with no visible legs or antennae, and attach tightly to the plant on which they are feeding.
Like aphids, scale insects also feed on plant sap, using their long, threadlike mouthparts.
Heavily infested plants grow poorly and may suffer dieback of twigs and branches.
As adult scales are protected from insecticides by waxy coverings, control measures must be aimed at unprotected immatures or the overwintering stage.
Armored scales are so named because they secrete a protective cover over their bodies.
Most species overwinter as eggs beneath the female cover.
In order to avoid treating a single insect under different hosts, the insects are presented in accordance with the zoological systematics already used by the present author.
In spring, eggs hatch into tiny mobile crawlers that migrate to new feeding sites.
After a few days, crawlers settle, insert their mouthparts, and begin feeding.
Large populations can build up unnoticed before plants begin to show visible symptoms.
In general, soft scales are larger and more convex than armored scales.
Soft scales usually cover themselves with wax, but they lack the detachable protective cover for which armored scales are named.
Most soft scales overwinter as immature, fertilized females.
This polyphagous insect, which is an important pest of citrus worldwide, is at present widely distributed in citrus-growing areas of Persia.
Only summary descriptions of important insects that damage field crops and fruit trees are reported.
The adult female of I. purchasi is a distinctive insect, being quite large (about 3.5 mm), with a brown body covered with a layer of wax.
Whiteflies are tiny, flying insects that derive their name from the mealy white wax covering their wings and body.
While adult whiteflies are similar in appearance, the immature stages are more distinctive.
Whiteflies suck phloem sap, causing leaves to wilt and drop when populations are large.
The members of this group may be found in both terrestrial and aquatic habitats.
All of them, however, have piercing-sucking mouthparts.
While some are predacious, others feed on plants, sucking their sap.
They are of special economic importance because they have toxic saliva that they inject into a plant, producing necrosis and the death of tissues.
This, in turn, may result, in severe cases, in the death of foliage and branches.
The most important member of this suborder, which frequently threatens wheat plantations in Persia is, without doubt, the senn pest, belonging to two genera i.e. Eurygaster and Aelia.
The members of this order, commonly known as thrips, are small insects, winged or wingless.
The winged species have two pairs of wings with few or no veins, fringed with long hairs.
Thrips have piercing-sucking mouthparts and undergo intermediate metamorphosis, accompanied with one or two inactive pupal instars.
While some feed on plants, they may also cause skin irritation in humans.
They also act as vectors of important plant viruses such as tomato spotted wilt virus and peanut yellow spot virus.
The latter species is an important, polyphagous insect attacking annual plants, in particular tobacco, onions, beans, sugar beets, tomatoes, potatoes, peas, and different cucurbits especially watermelons.
The leaves of plants attacked by T. tabaci are silvered and flecked. Heavy attacks lead to the wilting of young plants, and occasionally to the death of entire crops.
This order includes beetles and weevils characterized by having forewings that are thickened and hardened into elytra protecting the delicate, folded hind wings.
They undergo complete metamorphosis, and both larvae and adults possess mouthparts specialized for chewing.
Coleoptera is represented by numerous important economic species which feed on living plants, damaging roots, trunk, branches, buds, leaves, flowers, and fruits.
While some families, such as ladybirds, are mostly effective predators, many destructive pests of stored products belong to this order.
These larvae, which are known as white grubs, are easily recognized by their broad and fleshy appearance, white or grayish white color together with the C-shaped body, having well-developed thoracic legs rarely used for locomotion.
While larvae of some subfamilies attack above-ground parts of plants, the larvae of other subfamilies such as Melolontha melolontha are mainly root-feeding and in some cases may become exceedingly injurious.
The date palm fruit stalk borer, Oryctes elegans Prell, from the subfamily Dynastinae, is a major pest of the date palm in southern parts of Persia.
While the larvae feed on the petioles in the crown and on sap from the base of the fronds.
The adults feed on the fronds midribs and the rachis of inflorescence.
Winged species are strong fliers, but apterous and brachypterous species occur; and a subterranean nest is constructed for breeding purposes.
The larvae also attack the roots of sugar cane, causing serious damage in cane-growing regions of Persia.
It is also a very polyphagous species distributed in many regions of Persia, in particular in temperate zones.
It is considered an important pest of field crops and orchards.
The adult insects have often brilliant, metallic colorations, Although members of this family are typically inhabitants of hot moist forests, several genera such as Sphenoptera, and Capnodis are of significant importance throughout Persia.
The adult beetles, which visit flowers and feed on pollen and buds, may also gnaw at young branches and leaves.
The larvae, according to the species, may bore into the roots and/or burrow galleries in the trunk.
