18th and 19th Century- During this period the significance of agricultural research and agricultural education was negligible.
Foreign companies brought several crops and fruits and flowers of the new world.
The business men were interested in producing commercial crops such as indigo, cotton, jute, tea and sugarcane.
British period- During this period also no attention was paid on agriculture.
In 1906 Imperial agricultural institute, Pusa(Darbhanga) was established.
During 1905-1907 6 agricultural universities were opened in entire India, which consisted of Sabaur(Bihar), Layalpur(Punjab) now in Pakistan, Kanpur(Uttar Pradesh), Pune(Maharashtra), Coimbatore(Tamil nadu) and Nagpur(Maharashtra).
Second phase(1968-88)- It was called `` Green Revolution period `` .
Though good symptoms were seen in agriculture since 1965 which began under the development of 1940 decade in United States of america and spread in the world with other crops.
This revolution was seen in India under wheat and paddy during end of 1960 decade(1966-67).
The specialties were- advanced technologies, more capital investment and more agricultural production. The land became restricted but the production increased and providing food to the increasing human population became a major objective(which reached around 5 billion).
For the past 60 years since 1950-51 to 2010-11, there were several changes in the country , which were remarkable.
The contribution of agriculture in national gross product(GDP)has definitely reduced, it was 52.2% during 1950-51 and reduced to 14.1% during 2011-12 while during this period food grain increased from 50.8 m.l tonne to 224.78 m.l tonne which means there was 4 1/2 times growth. Wheat production increased up to 85.93 m.l tonnes.
There was 7 times increase in rice, 4 times increase in maize and 3 times increase in banana and sugarcane.
Apart from bringing self dependence on food front, records were made in the production of dairy, hens, fish rearing, vegetables, fruits etc.
Before independence we had to import 10-12 m.l tonnes food grain and today we are able to send them.
